# what is MAC Address.
               A MAC (Media Access Control) Address is a unique, hardware-assigned identifier 
               embedded directly into a device's Network Interface Card (NIC) at manufacturing. 
               Often called a physical or burned-in address, it allows network hardware to deliver 
               data locally to the correct device.

# Anatomy & Structure
               A standard MAC-48 address consists of 48 bits (6 bytes or 6 octets) and is represented
               in hexadecimal format.

               Format Examples: 00:1A:2B:3C:4D:5E (colon-delimited), 00-1A-2B-3C-4D-5E (hyphen-delimited),
               or 001A.2B3C.4D5E (dot-delimited).
Structure & Format
              A MAC address is a 48-bit (6-byte) binary number expressed as 12 hexadecimal characters, usually formatted in one of three ways:
              00:1A:2B:3C:4D:5E (Colon-delimited — most common)
              00-1A-2B-3C-4D-5E (Hyphen-delimited)
              001A.2B3C.4D5E (Dot-delimited — common in Cisco routers)
              The 48 bits are divided into two equal halves:

Field	                                        Size	                   Function
OUI (Organizationally Unique Identifier)	  First 24 bits (3 bytes)   	Identifies the hardware vendor (e.g., Apple, Intel, Cisco) assigned by the IEEE.
UAA (Universally Administered Address)    	Last 24 bits (3 bytes)	    Unique serial number assigned by the manufacturer to that specific card.
